<Base title="The horses"
      eyebrow="Cavalry, stables, a yard, and four generations after"
      dek="A cavalryman in Argentina, a trainer and racehorse owner at the Cape, stables at Rugby in 1931 — and four further generations of jockeys and trainers after him. The horse tradition was the truest thing the family remembered.">
<section class="blk">
<h2>The claim, and what became of it</h2>
  <p>
    <span class="chip doc">Confirmed</span> The family said he brought horse breeding and racing from
    Argentina to South Africa. Four documents, from four unconnected places, now say he was a horseman:
  </p>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:9rem">Date</th><th>Document</th><th>What it says</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="mono">1915</td><td>Argentine passport, military service</td>
          <td>Served in the <strong>Guardia Nacional, 6º de Caballería</strong> — the 6th Cavalry.
            A horseman in Argentina before he was anything at the Cape.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">1931</td><td>Cape Town municipal building file<br /><span class="small mono">KAB 3/CT 4/2/1/3/474, B796</span></td>
          <td>"Plans of proposed <strong>STABLES</strong>, Koeberg Road, <strong>Rugby</strong>. RP Lerena."</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">1940</td><td>Argentine passport, profession</td>
          <td><strong>Trainer</strong>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">1950</td><td>Union of South Africa death certificate<br /><span class="small mono">A 214036</span></td>
          <td><strong>Race Horse Owner</strong>.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    And in the debts of his estate, between a garage bill and an insurance premium, a claim from
    W. C. Blake for <strong>&pound;26 of carrots</strong>. <a href={u("/estate/")}>The estate &rarr;</a>
  </p>
  <p>
    <span class="chip inf">Still overstated</span> The literal claim — that South Africa had no racing
    before a Lerena arrived — cannot stand: organised racing at the Cape long predates him. What is now
    documented is narrower and sturdier: <strong>he was a horse-dealer, a trainer and an owner, he came out
    of Argentine cavalry, and he built stables at the Cape</strong>.
  </p>
  <div class="panel" style="margin:1.2rem 0">
    <p style="margin-top:0"><span class="chip lore">And the family says more than that</span></p>
    <p style="margin-bottom:0">
      <em>"Pablo had big stables in Cape Town… <strong>Pablo used to import horses into South Africa from
      Argentina for the ZA government</strong>. He then also brought in some horses for his own stables from
      there."</em>
    </p>
  </div>
  <p>
    That is <strong>exactly the testable form of the horse claim</strong> which this archive set out on its
    first day, before any of it was known — that he imported Argentine bloodstock, rather than inventing
    South African racing. It is family testimony and not yet a document, but it names something findable:
    <strong>government remount and bloodstock contracts</strong>, and import registrations with a country of
    origin.
  </p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>Four generations after him</h2>
  <p>
    <span class="chip">Public record</span> The South African racing press describes a Lerena dynasty, and
    its account of the earlier generations matches this archive's documents without having consulted them:
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    Gavin Lerena's <strong>great-grandfather and grandfather were trainers</strong>.
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    On the family's own chart that great-grandfather is <strong>Pablo Armando Lerena</strong> and that
    grandfather is his son <strong>Ricardo Juan Carlos</strong> (b. 1920). So an Argentine consular passport
    of 1940 and the modern racing press, with no connection whatever, call the same man a trainer.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Carlos "Tex" Lerena</strong> — a top jockey for 22 years, who also rode in Mauritius.
      Gavin's father; Ricardo's son.</li>
    <li><strong>Spike Lerena</strong> — former jockey, leading Gauteng trainer, trained
      <em>Jungle Rock</em>. Gavin's uncle.</li>
    <li><strong>Gavin Lerena</strong> — champion jockey. <strong>Stephen Lerena</strong>, Spike's son,
      trains at Randjesfontein; <strong>Brandon</strong> rides for the Ricky Maingard stable.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <span class="chip doc">Cross-confirmed</span> One detail deserves singling out. The racing press names
    <strong>Frederick Chapell</strong> — a jockey who trained <strong>Migraine</strong>, winner of the
    <strong>1957 Durban July</strong> — as a great-uncle of this family. The family tree supplies the
    connection: <strong>Ricardo Juan Carlos Lerena</strong> married <strong>Doreen May Chappell</strong>
    (1921&ndash;1999). Ricardo is Gavin's grandfather, so a Chappell of Doreen's generation is exactly a
    "great uncle" to Gavin.
  </p>
  <p>
    A family tree and the South African racing press, neither aware of the other, put a
    <strong>Chappell</strong> beside this family in the same generation. That is what corroboration looks
    like.
  </p>
  <div class="panel" style="margin:1.2rem 0">
    <p style="margin-top:0"><span class="chip doc">Resolved</span></p>
    <p style="margin-bottom:0">
      The two Chappells are one woman under two names. <strong>Rose Forbes</strong> had first been married
      to a <strong>Chappell</strong> and had a daughter, <strong>Doreen May Chappell</strong>. Rose then
      married <strong>Roque</strong> in 1936; Doreen married Roque's brother <strong>Ricardo</strong> in
      1941. <strong>Roque had married his own brother's mother-in-law</strong> — which is why the Corpus
      Christi annotation names his bride "Rosalina Wilhelmina Chappell", her name by the earlier marriage,
      and why the family remembered that the two brothers <em>"never spoke again"</em>.
    </p>
  </div>
  <p class="cap">
    Living people are named here only as public figures, from published racing sources. No relationship is
    asserted that a record does not carry. <a href={u("/method/")}>Method &rarr;</a>
  </p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>Gilberto Justiniano Lerena Lenguas</h2>
  <p><span class="chip doc">Documented</span></p>
  <p>
    The name is already written into Argentine racing at the highest level.
    <strong>Gilberto Justiniano Lerena Lenguas</strong> was a <strong>co-founder of the Argentine Stud
    Book</strong> — the national thoroughbred registry itself — and bred <strong>Old Man</strong>, winner of
    the Argentine Quadruple Crown. He was associated with Stud Oriental, Stud Bend Or, Haras La Guardia,
    Haras El Moro and Haras Las Ortigas, and owned his own <strong>Haras El Viejo</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    A Group 1 race still carries his name. The <strong>Gran Premio Gilberto Lerena</strong> is run at the
    <strong>Hipódromo Argentino de Palermo</strong> in Buenos Aires over 2,200 metres on turf, for fillies
    and mares of three years and up. It was founded in <strong>1905</strong> as the Premio Abril and
    <strong>renamed in his honour in 1914</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two details are worth holding onto. First, <strong>Lerena Lenguas</strong> is a River Plate double
    surname and <em>Lenguas</em> is Uruguayan — consistent with the name's Uruguayan density and with
    <em>Stud Oriental</em>, <em>oriental</em> being the ordinary Argentine word for Uruguayan. Second, the
    <strong>1914 renaming</strong> is the kind of honour usually paid at or just after a death, which
    brackets his life and gives the search a date to work around.
  </p>
  <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent);margin:1.4rem 0">
    <p class="lab" style="margin:0 0 8px">13 September 2026 &mdash; that guess turned out to be right,
      and it arrived from the parish register</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>The paragraph above was written on 8 September as an <em>inference</em>: that <em>Lenguas</em>
        looked Uruguayan, and that <em>Stud Oriental</em> was a Uruguayan&rsquo;s name for a stud. Five
        days later the Rosario baptism index was read end to end, 1731 to 1901, and the single Lerena
        in a hundred and seventy years turned out to be a child of
        <strong>Don Luis Lerena Lenguas</strong> and <strong>Do&ntilde;a Julia Juanic&oacute;</strong>
        &mdash; the act saying of both,
        <em><strong>&ldquo;naturales del E. Oriental&rdquo;</strong></em>.</p>
      <p>Published Uruguayan genealogy makes that Luis a <strong>brother of Gilberto</strong>: both sons
        of <strong>Luis Lerena y Fern&aacute;ndez (1811&ndash;1878)</strong> and
        <strong>Justiniana Lenguas Gonz&aacute;lez</strong> of Montevideo. And the child baptised at
        Rosario carries <strong>Justiniana</strong> as her fifth name, for that grandmother.</p>
      <p>So the turf and the parish register arrive at the same Montevideo house from opposite ends. It
        remains what it was &mdash; <strong>a lead of real weight, and no relationship</strong>. But the
        guess about <em>Stud Oriental</em> was not a guess for very long.
        <a href={u("/hypotheses/")}>Hypothesis 1c &rarr;</a> &middot;
        <a href={u("/searched/")}>What was read &rarr;</a></p>
      <p class="cap">The 1866 act is primary and was read at full resolution.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent);margin:1.4rem 0">
    <p class="lab" style="margin:0 0 8px">13 September 2026, later that day &mdash; the brotherhood
      moved out of the published genealogy and into the registers</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>The paragraph above says <em>&ldquo;published Uruguayan genealogy makes that Luis a brother of
        Gilberto&rdquo;</em>, and footnotes it as <strong>secondary</strong> &mdash; Geneanet, Geni, a
        blog. That footnote is now out of date.</p>
      <p><strong>Two Uruguayan parish records, entered for two different children of Gilberto&rsquo;s own,
        give his parents as Luis and Justiniana Lenguas.</strong> They were found by searching not the
        surname but the <em>mother</em> &mdash; <em>Justiniana Lenguas</em> &mdash; which is the only
        stable name in a family the clerks and the indexers between them write as Lerena, Lorena,
        <em>Serena</em>, Languas and Lenquas. The same eight rows returned four siblings:
        <strong>Justinia</strong> (1843), <strong>Luis Eugenio</strong> (1843),
        <strong>Alberto Otario</strong> (1850), <strong>Gilberto</strong> (c.1854) and
        <strong>Mar&iacute;a Ema</strong> (1857).</p>
      <p>And the same search found <strong>Luis Eugenio&rsquo;s death</strong> &mdash; 24 December 1901,
        act 216, image read &mdash; which names his father <em>Luis Lerena</em>, already dead, and his
        mother <em>Justiniana Lenguas</em>, <strong>alive at seventy-nine and in the house</strong>. It
        also gives his profession: <em><strong>hacendado</strong></em>. A landowner. The first statement
        of this family&rsquo;s standing anywhere in the archive &mdash; and a man of land and horses is a
        rather different neighbour for a stud than a name on a blog.</p>
      <div class="note" style="margin-top:14px"><strong>So the two ends have met, and it is no longer a
        coincidence of surname.</strong> The man who co-founded the Argentine Stud Book and the man whose
        daughter was the single Lerena baptised at Rosario in a hundred and seventy years are
        <strong>brothers</strong>, out of one Montevideo household.</div>
      <p class="cap" style="margin-top:14px">The paragraph that stood here said <em>&ldquo;and it is still
        not primary &mdash; neither image has been opened&rdquo;</em>. That was true when it was written
        and false about an hour later, and it stood for a day. The image <strong>was</strong> opened.
        See below. It remains true that none of this finds Juan Carlos, who is in none of those five
        siblings, and that it does not connect Pablo Armando to any of them.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent);margin:1.4rem 0">
    <p class="lab" style="margin:0 0 8px">Act nº 1447 &mdash; the priest&rsquo;s own hand</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>San Francisco de As&iacute;s, Ciudad Vieja, Montevideo, folio 504, <strong>31 October 1886</strong>,
        in the hand of <strong>Mart&iacute;n P&eacute;rez</strong>. The baptism of Gilberto&rsquo;s daughter
        <strong>Mar&iacute;a Ang&eacute;lica Julia</strong>:</p>
      <blockquote class="rec" style="margin:14px 0"><em>&hellip;bautic&eacute; solemnemente a Mar&iacute;a
        Ang&eacute;lica Julia&hellip; hija leg&iacute;tima de Don <strong>Gilberto Lerena</strong> y de
        Do&ntilde;a <strong>Julia Salvanach</strong>, <strong>orientales</strong>: abuelos paternos: Don
        <strong>Luis</strong> y Do&ntilde;a <strong>Justiniana Lenguas</strong>: maternos: Don
        Crist&oacute;bal, Do&ntilde;a Angela Alvarez: fueron padrinos: Don Rufino Yurramendi y Do&ntilde;a
        <strong>Julia Juanic&oacute; de Lerena</strong></em></blockquote>
      <p><strong>The paternal grandparents are the couple named as Luis Eugenio&rsquo;s parents</strong> in
        his marriage act of 1864 and his death act of 1901. So the brotherhood is no longer an inference
        from two index entries: it is what a priest wrote down while the family stood in front of him.</p>
      <div class="note" style="margin-top:14px"><strong>And look who the godmother is.</strong>
        <em>Do&ntilde;a Julia Juanic&oacute; de Lerena</em> &mdash; Luis Eugenio&rsquo;s own wife, standing
        at the font for her husband&rsquo;s brother&rsquo;s child. These were not two households that
        happened to share grandparents. They were one family, in one parish, acting as one.</div>
      <p class="cap" style="margin-top:14px">Two new people out of the same line: <strong>Don
        Crist&oacute;bal</strong> and <strong>Do&ntilde;a Angela Alvarez</strong>, Julia Salvanach&rsquo;s
        parents, who had never appeared in this archive before.</p>
      <p class="cap"><strong>And a second act says it too.</strong> The baptism of another of his sons,
        <strong>Ra&uacute;l Alfredo Luis</strong>, at the <strong>Catedral Bas&iacute;lica de Montevideo
        on 10 September 1885</strong>, gives the same grandparents in a different year, a different
        parish and a different hand. Two registers agreeing is a different thing from one register read
        twice. <a href={u("/searched/")}>What was read &rarr;</a></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="panel" style="margin:1.4rem 0">
    <p class="lab" style="margin:0 0 8px">His household &mdash; and the year the horses crossed the river</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Searching Gilberto <em>as a father</em> across the Argentine collections &mdash; which had never
        been done here &mdash; gives <strong>eight children</strong> by Julia Salvanach, and they fall
        into two halves:</p>
      <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:12px">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Born at Montevideo</th><th>Born at Buenos Aires</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Gilberto, 1878</td><td>Mar&iacute;a Carlota, bapt. <strong>29 Oct 1890</strong>, Recoleta</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Carlos, 1884 &mdash; bapt. Iglesia del Cord&oacute;n</td><td>Luis Felipe, bapt. 1892, Constituci&oacute;n</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Ra&uacute;l Alfredo Luis, 10 July 1885</td><td>C&eacute;sar Alberto, 1896</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Mar&iacute;a Ang&eacute;lica Julia, bapt. <strong>31 Oct 1886</strong></td><td>Horacio Alfredo, bapt. 1898, Recoleta</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="note" style="margin-top:14px"><strong>So the crossing is bracketed by baptisms rather
        than inferred from census birthplaces.</strong> The last child christened at Montevideo is
        <strong>31 October 1886</strong>; the first christened at Buenos Aires is <strong>29 October
        1890</strong>. This archive has said &ldquo;between 1887 and 1889&rdquo;, read off the switch in
        stated birthplaces. The baptisms are a little wider and a good deal firmer: they are records of
        the family, not of what its children later said about themselves.</div>
      <p class="cap" style="margin-top:14px"><strong>And Juan Carlos is not among the eight.</strong> That
        is worth saying plainly, because this was the best remaining place to put him &mdash; a Lerena
        household of the right surname, in the right country, at the right time, descending from the one
        Uruguayan house this archive has documented.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="panel" style="margin:1.2rem 0">
    <p style="margin:0">
      <strong>The archive claims no relationship.</strong> With a surname borne by only 2,124 people
      worldwide, a documented Lerena at the centre of Argentine breeding, in the same country and the same
      pursuit the family remembers, is a lead of real weight. It is not a lead that may be assumed into a
      fact. Rarity makes a connection <em>likely</em>; only a document makes it <em>true</em>.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>Lerena in South African racing</h2>
  <p>
    <span class="chip doc">Documented</span> The name is also present in South African racing today —
    <strong>Gavin Lerena</strong> is among the country's leading jockeys, riding at Turffontein and the
    major Highveld and Cape meetings.
  </p>
  <p>
    When this page was first written the archive claimed no relationship, on the grounds that a shared rare
    surname is a proposal and not a proof. The family's own descendant chart has since supplied the
    descent — Pablo Armando &rarr; Ricardo &rarr; Carlos "Tex" &rarr; Gavin — and the racing press
    independently describes the same succession of trainers. The
    <a href={u("/south-africa/")}>founder-cluster reading</a> was right.
  </p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>How this gets settled</h2>
  <div class="grid g2">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">The stud books</h3>
      <p style="margin-bottom:0">
        The <strong>South African Stud Book</strong> and the Jockey Club registers record owners, breeders,
        farms and — critically — <strong>imported horses with their country of origin</strong>. An Argentine
        import under a Lerena name would be conclusive. The <strong>Argentine Stud Book</strong> is the
        matching volume at the other end.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Racing calendars and the press</h3>
      <p style="margin-bottom:0">
        Owners' colours, race cards and results were printed weekly in both countries. A single card naming
        a Lerena as owner or breeder dates his presence and often his farm.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Haras El Viejo</h3>
      <p style="margin-bottom:0">
        Gilberto Lerena's own stud. Its records, sales and dispersal — and any family succession — are the
        direct route into his household, and thence to whether it connects here.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Shipping</h3>
      <p style="margin-bottom:0">
        Live bloodstock moved by sea under documentation. Cargo manifests and quarantine records between
        Buenos Aires and Cape Town would show an importation directly.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
